# Benchmark endpoint plumbing (doc 05 D1). Copy to benchmarks/.env and fill in,
# or just `export` these in your shell before running a benchmark.
#
# Each harness talks to an OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint via these three vars;
# the harness "model" argument is openai/$BENCH_MODEL (or the harness's own
# spelling of an OpenAI-compatible model id).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# L2 — local canonical (default): the llama-server started by
#   pixi r -e llamacpp-source-cuda start-server
# BENCH_MODEL is your models.ini preset name; record it in the ledger
# model.preset. Record any non-API-visible server tweaks in model.config.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPENAI_BASE_URL="http://localhost:8080/v1"
OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-local"           # llama-server ignores the value
BENCH_MODEL="Qwen3.6-35B-A3B"

# Precision label recorded verbatim in the ledger model.precision (doc 00).
# Any value incl. "unknown" is valid — the point is that it is written down.
BENCH_PRECISION="IQ4_XS+q8_0kv"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# L1 — user-designated reference endpoint (precision explicitly labelled).
# Point the same three vars at the reference endpoint when running an L1 arm.
# No vendor certification is required; the precision label is your assertion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPENAI_BASE_URL="https://<vendor>/v1"
# OPENAI_API_KEY="<your-key>"
# BENCH_MODEL="<reference-model-id>"
# BENCH_PRECISION="BF16"            # or FP16 / unknown / ...
